[10/200] Running: What is model parallelism and how does it help scale large m...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Learn how this technique balances GPU memory usage, increases GPU utilization, and enables you to build, train, and run larger models.

## What is model parallelism?

Model parallelism is a distributed training technique that splits deep learning models across multiple GPUs to scale large models. Similar to data parallelism, this technique is primarily helpful when training very large models that exhaust GPU memory when distributed training is not set up.

Data parallelism is a technique that distributes copied deep learning models across multiple GPUs, where input batch data is split into chunks so that each GPU processes a different data segment using the same model. With model parallelism, each model layer is spread across multiple GPUs. However, instead of each GPU working on a different data segment, the input batch data is processed on each GPU in tandem according to the order of the layers in the model (figure 1). It’s important to note that data parallelism can’t be used to scale up a single model past a single GPU’s memory limit, whereas you CAN use model parallelism to train very large models on a multi-GPU system.

Data parallelism and model parallelism can be used together. This is known as hybrid parallelism. Figure 1 provides an overview of how model parallelism and data parallelism work together in a multi-GPU system.

In this illustration, samples are distributed across GPUs using data parallelism to increase the effective batch size. Each GPU then processes its samples simultaneously. You can distribute individual layers across a number of GPUs, which is known as model parallelism. The order that the layers are placed on the GPUs may not correspond to the sequence of how they are used, because layer dependencies might determine how layers are placed. In this illustration, the GPUs are used as a single virtual GPU, so pipelining and gradient synchronization are not required.

## How does model parallelism work?

Model parallelism breaks up very large models that exhaust GPU memory into smaller pieces to fit within a GPU’s memory constraints. This allows training to start even before all data is loaded onto a single GPU. Rather than dividing the mini-batch across GPUs, the work required to evaluate the model is distributed using a different type of input pipeline: a parameter server architecture on the CPU or horizontal model parallelism across various GPUs. Fully connected layers typically use the deterministic pipeline architecture at the CPU level, whereas convolutional layers process parameter gradients on all GPUs.

### Hardware requirements

To implement model parallelism, you have to consider using a batch pipeline or a loop parallelism pattern. The batch pipeline pattern is best used when working with models that have layers that require large intermediate or final output tensors. But pipeline parallelism can be challenging to set up because it requires consistent processing times on each GPU. Horizontal model parallelism, or loop parallelism, is more flexible when working with GPU requirements. Considerations for horizontal model parallelism include:

- The number of GPUs and GPUs used per model layer (figure 2).
- GPU memory size, number of GPU cores, and the amount of GPU memory available.
- Latency-sensitive applications require very fast interconnects with low latency and high bandwidth. NVLink 3.0 interconnects can be as fast as 600 GB/s. Not all GPUs support this feature.
- System bandwidth to move data in and out.

Figure 2A. The first GPUs redistribute parameter gradients horizontally. Figure 2B. Horizontal model parallelism balances processing the gradients of the layers before continuing.

#### TorchScript and XLA

Google TensorFlow provides support for XLA, which is a machine learning compiler that uses a just-in-time (JIT) compilation approach to replace standard TensorFlow operations. In a similar manner, TorchScript simplifies the process for extracting models to a platform-independent format from PyTorch code for JIT compilation and/or ahead-of-time (AOT) compilation to run on target hardware. To achieve comparable levels of performance, TorchScript and XLA need a similar set of requirements in terms of the type of model and amount of shared memory across GPUs available for use.

