Encoder-decoder models consist of two separate neural networks: an encoder and a decoder. The encoder takes in an input sequence, processes it, and outputs a fixed-length vector representation. The decoder then takes this vector representation as input and generates an output sequence. This architecture is commonly used in tasks such as machine translation, where the encoder processes the source language sentence and the decoder generates the corresponding target language sentence.

Decoder-only models, on the other hand, only consist of a single decoder network. These models are typically used for tasks such as language modeling, where the goal is to predict the next word in a sequence given the previous words. The decoder processes the input sequence and outputs a probability distribution over the vocabulary for each position in the sequence.

One key difference between encoder-decoder and decoder-only models is that encoder-decoder models have a separate encoder network that can be pre-trained on large amounts of data, allowing the model to learn useful representations of the input data that can be transferred to downstream tasks. Decoder-only models, on the other hand, do not have this separate encoder network and must learn to represent the input data and generate the output sequence simultaneously.

Another difference is that encoder-decoder models are often used for tasks where the input and output sequences have different lengths, such as machine translation. Decoder-only models, on the other hand, are typically used for tasks where the input and output sequences have the same length, such as language modeling.
