The Pre-Trained Language Models

Pre-trained language models are popular certainties in natural language processing applications. Some commonly used language models are BERT, RoBERTa, XLNet, T5, and the list goes on and on. The essential idea is to design a task-agnostic model using massive data sources so that good representations of word meanings can be learned during the pre-training phase. These learned representations are then used to initialize the weights for the subsequent downstream tasks. Interestingly, recent results showed that substantial performance improvement could occur when applying the single pre-trained model (e.g., BERT) to multiple downstream tasks. For example, researchers found that on downstream tasks such as Sentiment Analysis, Question Answering, and Natural Language Inference, the performance of simple fine-tuning with a BERT base model was quite impressive without adding more features or engineering customized components.

This result implies that large pre-trained models are versatile and can be easily adapted to fit the requirements for the downstream tasks with minimal changes to the model architecture. Therefore, it makes a lot of sense to (1) build a large pre-trained model on the massive data sources at a scale, (2) fine-tune the pre-trained models for downstream tasks using more targeted data which are usually significantly smaller than large data sources.

Transfer Learning

Transfer learning is the process by which a model trained on one task, called the base task, is reused as the starting point for a model on a second task, called the target task. This forms a type of multi-task learning framework, where the pre-trained models are applied to new tasks with novel data in various forms through a fine-tuning phase. There are a variety of beneficial effects of transfer in which models may construe from features that are shared across tasks in terms of architectural components and parameters.

Transfer learning has proven to be extremely useful for many problems, especially in computer vision (CV) applications. Since similar CV models have the same input formats such as image field size, it has been easy to apply the pre-trained models to existing tasks. However, the methods are less accepted in NLP, primarily because the tasks are so disparate and the formats vary a lot across domains and applications.

