What is the difference between benchmarking and profiling? The terms are often used interchangeably.

- Benchmarking is done to evaluate performance, generally by running tests against pre-determined reference points and comparing the result of its actual performance. It can be done through a testing suite specific to an operating system or software. Benchmarking is highly relevant for rating or ranking hardware or a service, as compared to another.
- Profiling, on the other hand, is the process of evaluating the performance of a program or system on specific inputs/scenarios where the profile is created against the program/system on hand and provides a detailed analysis of the runtime characteristics of the software.

Inference micro-benchmark can be run using:

- Tensorflow Serving
- NVIDIA TensorRT
- AWS SageMaker
- AzureML
- GCP AI Platform Predictions

Most micro-benchmarks focus on:

- Measuring latency and throughput

In deep learning micro-benchmarking, often you won't find benefits from pinning to core. It is because most layers/ops have to
access global memory. Therefore there is little GPU-core to core contention.

For inference throughput

- Easy to start by using a unified model.
- Complex production models are composed of multiple models, sometimes multiple model types mixed together.
- Trained models from multiple vendors. A model can include multiple smaller models, combined together by some kind of wrapper in order to perform an end-to-end inference.

For inference throughput, it is important to understand the types of model that are being benchmarked. Looking at a single model will lead to incomplete conclusions in terms of observed throughput. This is particularly relevant in a microservices world (real-time ML as a service), where you will encounter multiple accesses to the model in parallel streams leading to mixed model loads on the server.

Inference throughput can be affected by:

- Model architecture.
- Input data size.
- Input data types and format.
- Precision of computation.
- Output post-processing of results.
- Loading/unloading of models.
- Prefetching of data for batches.
- Multithreading and concurrency.
- Core affinity and scheduling. The position of GPU cores in a commodity chip can have a significantly different thermal profile and impact performance at high load.
- Memory and bandwidth management.

Tips :

You'll likely need to benchmark for quite a few GPU utilization levels to get a reference for your model accuracy to GPU performance tradeoff.

