[56/200] Running: Explain how masked self-attention works in BERT....

🧠 LLM Response:


Masked self-attention in BERT is a mechanism used during the pre-training phase to encourage the model to learn meaningful contextual relationships between words in a sentence.

In BERT, the input sequence is first tokenized into subwords using WordPiece. Then, a special token [CLS] is added at the beginning of the sequence, and a special token [SEP] is added at the end of the sequence to indicate the separation between sentences, if there are two sentences.

During the pre-training phase, the masked self-attention mechanism is applied to the input sequence to randomly mask out some of the input tokens. Specifically, 15% of the input tokens are randomly selected, and 80% of those selected tokens are replaced with a [MASK] token, 10% are replaced with a random token, and 10% are kept unchanged.

The [MASK] token is a special token that is used to indicate that the corresponding token has been masked out. During the pre-training phase, the model learns to predict the masked token based on the context provided by the surrounding tokens. This helps the model to learn the meaning of each token in the context of the sentence.

The self-attention mechanism in BERT is a multi-head attention mechanism that computes the attention scores between each pair of tokens in the input sequence. The attention scores are used to weigh the importance of each token in the sequence when computing the contextual representation of each token.

The masked self-attention mechanism is used during the pre-training phase to encourage the model to learn contextual relationships between the tokens in the input sequence, even when some of the tokens are masked out. This helps the model to learn more robust representations of the input sequence that can be used for a wide range of downstream NLP tasks.

In summary, masked self-attention in BERT is a mechanism used during the pre-training phase to randomly mask out some of the input tokens and encourage the model to learn meaningful contextual relationships between the remaining tokens in the input sequence. This helps the model to learn robust representations of the input sequence that can be used for a wide range of downstream NLP tasks.
