[64/200] Running: What is model checkpointing and resumption?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 
=============================================

Model checkpointing is the way to save the state of a model to the storage.
This is typically done at the end of each epoch and before evaluation so
that we can later continue training from this model state. The benefits of
model checkpointing include:

- to save disk space when the model has small improvements over the current
  best model over multiple epochs (saving the model the end of each epoch
  and discarding worse models),
- to speedup the training process by starting training from the state of a
  saved model (in case training uses random initialization per epoch),
- to separate evaluation (and possibly hyperparameter tuning such as scheduling
  learning rate) from training so as not to slow down training (model evaluation is
  incomparably slower than training, e.g., due to reading data for evaluation),
- to save a model before the training ends (e.g. in case it is stopped by humans).


Continuing training of the model from the state saved by the checkpoint is
called model resumption. This, in turn are used for the following purposes:

- to continue training when training process ends unexpectedly,
- to continue training when the model overfits the data.

