[65/200] Running: Describe how checkpoint sharding works in distributed traini...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Checkpoint sharding is a technique to shard single checkpoints of model and optimizer states to make the model state fit in the parameter server memory. This checkpoint sharding technique makes it possible to train larger model that doesn’t fit in memory, with minimal modification in training code.

Checkpoint sharding is a technique used in distributed training to reduce the memory requirements of storing the model and optimizer states on the parameter server. In this technique, a single checkpoint is divided or sharded into multiple smaller checkpoints, each of which can fit into the memory of the parameter server.

The process of checkpoint sharding works as follows:

1. At the beginning of each training iteration, the master node sends the sharded checkpoints to the available worker nodes.
2. Each worker node loads the sharded checkpoints into its local memory and initializes its local model and optimizer states.
3. The worker nodes perform the forward and backward passes of the training algorithm using their local model and optimizer states.
4. After the backward pass, the worker nodes update their local model and optimizer states and communicate the required updates to the parameter server.
5. The parameter server aggregates the updates received from the worker nodes and updates the global model and optimizer states.
6. The parameter server then sends the updated sharded checkpoints back to the worker nodes, which load them into their local memory for the next training iteration.

Checkpoint sharding makes it possible to train larger models that don’t fit in the memory of the parameter server, with minimal modification in the training code. It helps to overcome the memory constraints of the parameter server and enables distributed training of larger models.

In summary, checkpoint sharding is a technique to shard single checkpoints of model and optimizer states to make the model state fit in the parameter server memory. It reduces the memory requirements of storing the model and optimizer states on the parameter server, enabling distributed training of larger models with minimal modification in the training code.
