From its invention in 1956 by Frank Rosenblatt, artificial neural networks became significant components of AI. Since then, feedforward architectures of millions of neurons, inspired by the mammalian vision system, have set standards in performance in many challenges regarding image classification. Recent developments culminated in deep networks with tens to hundreds of layers and have achieved unprecedented performance levels.

Despite being inspired by the mammalian visual system, deep models fail dramatically in several aspects addressed by adaptive algorithms in biology. In such biological models, while neurons at the earliest stages of processing search the whole field of vision, downstream neurons observed in the thalamus and the cortex have massive receptive fields with thousands of neurons. In other words, for every neuron looking at the human eye's center, there are thousands more neurons whose receptive field captures peripheral vision regions. As we will show in this post, this distributed allocation of the perceptual task has significant advantages regarding data efficiency and adaptive perception of the world.

Here is the core reason for inefficiency in computational models: deep convolutional networks, which currently generate top-performance in machine perception, require large amounts of data and tuning, and lack real-time adaptive capabilities. Surprisingly, these issues have attracted limited attention compared to relatively minor ones, such as gradient-vanishing, or use of the ReLU nonlinearity in residual connections -- the attention bottleneck perspective reviewed here shows that these seemingly minor concerns are in fact major factors limiting the potential of deep models in real-world applications.

Deep models are inefficient because they perform redundant and wasteful computations, and fail to distribute decision-making processes over multiple systemic estimators. A solution based on a distributed-adaptive framework is presented in our earlier work.

Why do feedforward networks follow a  pyramidal bottleneck architecture?

Downstream neurons of biological models usually cover disproportionally broad regions of the visual field -- in fact, the number of neurons in each layer grows super-linearly with the layer's depth. This phenomenon is explained by the hierarchical self-similar design of the mammalian cortex: big problems are recursively broken into smaller ones, or, stated differently, the 'broad' information on the previous layer is split into many smaller 'broader' problems to be independently handled.

