 How can deep learning models be deployed on the edge? How is edge intelligence different from cloud-based intelligence? We will try to address these questions on this post.

## Edge Artificial Intelligence

Edge intelligence is a type of computing that happens on edge devices, which are systems comprising sensors, microcontrollers, and actuators, e.g., Raspberry Pi, Arduino. Edge devices are connected to the internet and consume and process data. Edge AI combines edge intelligence and cloud intelligence to solve problems at the edge. The main goal of Edge AI is to enable devices to implement sophisticated algorithms that can quickly process high volumes of data, without consuming too much power and bandwidth.

Since many of the algorithms used in autonomous systems and IoT devices are powered by deep learning, edge AI frameworks need to enable deploying large neural networks on the edge. At the same time, edge AI frameworks need to allow for both optimizations in power consumption and use.

Deep learning models tend to consume a lot of computing power and memory, and they need to be trained on a server. This requires transferring data to the server, which in turn requires internet connectivity and consumes bandwidth. However, not all edge devices are connected to the internet or have constant connectivity. Therefore, it’s essential to have a solution that can enable the deployment and execution of machine learning models on edge devices, even when they are not connected. Decentralizing AI processing tasks in this way also has security advantages, as data doesn’t need to be sent to the cloud.

Deploying a neural network to an edge device involves two essential steps. First, you need to compress the model, so it fits in the memory of the edge device. Then, you optimize the execution so that it consumes the least amount of power, which is usually the most expensive resource on such devices. The process can be automated, so the framework takes model architectures as input, goes through a series of optimizations, and generates compact, fast, and accurate models that can be deployed on edge devices.

This post will cover the top five edge AI frameworks: OpenMMLab, TensorFlow Lite, TensorRT, EdgeDNN, and YOLO. We will explain each framework’s technology and architecture, and we will highlight its pros and cons.

## OpenMMLab

OpenMMLab provides an open-source platform for building computer vision applications. OpenMMLab was initially created to balance between research and production build for computer vision models, but now includes many applications, models, and toolsets. Some applications include celebrities recognition, dense optical flow, lane detection, video super resolution. Not all the applications feature edge AI capabilities.

OpenMMLab is based on an open-source framework called MMCV (openMMLab Computer Vision). MMCV is a flexible, lightweight, and easy-to-use framework for building computer vision applications on top of PyTorch. MMCV provides various data augmentation classes and lots of pre-built modules for computer vision. It also has a library for building pipelines, which can be used to develop custom computer vision models.

OpenMMLab supports multiple platforms, including Windows, Linux, Mac, and Android. It comes with various pre-built tools for installing the framework, visualizing the models’ outputs, and deploying models on edge devices. One of the advantages of OpenMMLab is that it supports a wide variety of models, including popular ones like YOLO, ResNet, and Max-DeepTL, as well as custom models.

OpenMMLab also includes pre-built packages for building Android and iOS applications. Overall, OpenMMLab offers a comprehensive set of tools for building computer vision models, but it is not explicitly optimized for edge devices.

## TensorFlow Lite

TensorFlow Lite (TFLite) is an open-source framework for deploying deep learning models on edge devices. TFLite uses TensorFlow to train the models, then converts the models into a compact form that can be executed in TensorFlow Lite runtime.

TFLite provides various optimization techniques for reducing the model’s size, such as quantization, which converts model weights from floating-point format to integer format. TFLite also offers quantization awareness training, enabling to direct the model to use lower-precision weights during training and inference.

TFLite can optimize the model’s execution further by analyzing the target hardware. Therefore, when optimizing, TFLite can select the most optimal data representation and execution paths based on the available hardware instructions and data types.

