🧠 LLM Response:


Rotary embeddings are a technique used in machine learning to represent categorical data as continuous vectors in a high-dimensional space. The idea behind rotary embeddings is to use a trainable rotation matrix to transform the one-hot encoded categorical data into a dense vector representation. This representation can then be used as input to a neural network.

The key principle behind rotary embeddings is the use of a rotation matrix to transform the one-hot encoded data. A rotation matrix is a square matrix that preserves the length of vectors and the angles between them. By using a rotation matrix to transform the one-hot encoded data, rotary embeddings preserve the structural relationships between the categories.

Rotary embeddings are trained using backpropagation and stochastic gradient descent. The rotation matrix is updated during training to minimize the loss function of the neural network. By optimizing the rotation matrix, rotary embeddings can learn to represent the categorical data in a way that is more useful for the task at hand.

Rotary embeddings have several advantages over other techniques for representing categorical data. They are able to capture the structural relationships between categories, which can be important for tasks such as natural language processing and recommendation systems. Rotary embeddings are also efficient to compute and require less memory than other techniques, such as word embeddings.

In summary, rotary embeddings are a technique for representing categorical data as continuous vectors in a high-dimensional space. They use a trainable rotation matrix to transform one-hot encoded data into a dense vector representation, preserving the structural relationships between categories. Rotary embeddings are trained using backpropagation and stochastic gradient descent, and have advantages over other techniques for representing categorical data.
