🧠 LLM Response:
 Well, folks, just how did Abby and Brittany Hensel become one of the world's most famous conjoined twins, making roughly $200,000 as teachers with two salaries and proof of their mental and physical prowess? Or how did the beautiful and otherworldly Elizabeth Holmes defraud millions of investors and go from being a student at Stanford to being on TIME's 100 Most Influential People List to a staggering total of 11 felony counts and now barely two years in prison?

Life is full of existential queries, and as generative AI continues to propel itself into the ether of our day-to-day lives, determining the best sampling methods in machine learning will only become more crucial for those seeking to make sense of themselves and this crazy, beautiful world we inhabit, all in the hopes of cobbling together a semblance of our place and purpose in the universe.

This article will explore these two popular sampling methods, top-p sampling and nucleus sampling, and how they differ in terms of their methods, applications, and results. We will also break down some lesser-known algorithms like modified CTGAN, residual sampling, and exponential sampling, all of which have paved their own way as niche sampling methods for specific use cases. Ultimately, we will expound upon the evolution of sampling methods in machine learning, and why these algorithms are not only important for technology but also for each of us as individual co-conspirators in this digital renaissance.

## A Generative Model Overview

The term "generative model" refers to a class of probabilistic models capable of generating new observations on their own. In other words, they approximate the underlying probability density over the data.

Impressively, generative models excel in domains like translation, text generation, speech synthesis, image-to-image translation, data imputation, and text to image generation. Backpropagation through stochastic variational inference, contrastive divergence, wake-sleep, and the multinomial distribution are all traditional approaches that PTMs (pretrained models) rely upon. However, several new sampling methods like top-p and nucleus sampling are improving these old methods significantly.

Before starting, we want to remind readers of the principle of generative models in natural language processing (NLP).

Generative models estimate P(y|x), which is the conditional probability of word y given context x.

(Generative Model Diagram from Hugging Face)

## Basic Sampling Methods in Machine Learning

We will discuss two prevalent sampling algorithm methods in AI and NLP: top-k sampling and nucleus sampling.

We also discuss two other methods, residual sampling and exponential sampling, that both home in for specific applications.

### Greedy Sampling

Greedy sampling is the simplest sampling algorithm in which you pick the most probable candidate given the previous choices. This method, however, does not take into account the future words and their mutual information.

### Random Sampling

Random sampling selects a token at random given the previous choices, paying no attention to word probabilities distribution. This method disregards word (token) frequency and the model itself.

### Top-k Sampling

Top-k sampling is based on below idea. Select the sample with the highest probability accordingly by sampling k tokens with the highest probabilities.

The probability of choosing a word is:

$$P(y) = \sum_{i=1}^k P(v_i)$$

where $k$ is the number of tokens picked based on their probability.

We walk through the `top_k_sampling` function, which is mostly based on the code and installation provided in this Hugging Face tutorial.

```
def top_k_sampling(model, context, candidate_word_count, k):
	with torch.no_grad():
		context = context.view(1, -1)
		input_ids = model.generate_special_token(context)
		next_candidate_word_scores, next_token_logits = model.generate(
			input_ids=input_ids,
			max_length=config.target_seq_len - len(input_ids),
			num_beams=config.infer_beam_state,
			early_stopping=True,
		)

	top_k_sampling_distribution = torch.topk
