🧠 LLM Response:
 Why are they important, and how exactly do they help? I'd like to take a bit of a deeper look at these questions in this post.

Transformers dominate state-of-the-art in Natural Language Processing(NLP). They are based on the attention mechanism which allows every token (word, sentence etc) to interact with every other token. This is fundamentally different to RNN(Recurrent Neural Network) architectures like LSTMs(Long Short-Term Memory) that use of a fixed context size. Transformers, on the other hand, allow long range interactions. Admittedly, self-attention can be computationally expensive, so some sort of lossy approximation is incorporated for practicality's sake. This is where sparsity, blockwise, and locality come into play. But this deep-dive focusses on interpreting the role of residual connections in transformer architectures.

Motivations for this post stem from several blog posts I've encountered that purported that residual connections allowed deep networks to learn/train/optimize/converge. Residual connections are an architectural design in neural networks, introduced around the same time Google released it's ResNet architecture for classification in ImageNet competition in 2015. While it's true that error gradients flow through the architecture more easily with residual connections, attempting to cloak them in the mystic light of magic - suggesting that they somehow allow Deep NNs to work when they otherwise wouldn't be able to, is iffy. Residual connections, in essence, slow training down compared to non-residual-connection architectures for optimizing learning parameters, and offer slightly improved generalization performance. This, indeed, warrants some discussion around why this is so.

So before we delve into transformers, a primer on the role of residual connections.

###### Why residual connections?

With the emergence of deep networks, requiring in the hundreds of layers for tasks like object detection and machine translation, the problem of gradient explosion and vanishing was real. To counter it, He et al. introduced skip connections in the form of residual connections to aid information flow through larger model parameter depths.

For the sake of concreteness and rigorousness in this "WHY" section, let's take a look at the value proposition of skip connections.

Gradient Flow through skip connections: Residual connections, in essence, provide an alternate source of information that already is provided through the path from input to fully-connected output layers. This allows the error gradient to flow through these extra layer of depth. The term gradient with residual connections is somewhat less accurate. The parameter updates are in the form of a moving average of gradient descents, akin to momentum and thus can be characterized as "momentum along the descent towards a global minimum". Instead, better formulation in terms of disparate units would be to work with optimization parameters (weights and biases) rather than gradients - which shows just exactly how these connections affect the parameter optimization process. Consider a simple feed-forward network with layers , where the output of the last layer is of the form:

