## Tensor Datatypes

To better understand the differences between BFloat16 and Float16 it is first helpful to understand the current Tensor datatypes that exist, as discussed earlier there are primarily 3 with the following properties:

Float32 — Also known as a float or single-precision float, the float32 has a 23-bit mantissa and 8-bit exponent, 24-bit total, giving it a precision of about 7 decimal digits representing the most commonly used data type, corresponding to the 32-bit x86 word length.

Float64 — Also known as a double-precision float, the float64 has a 53-bit mantissa with 11-bit exponent and 52 bits total, providing great precision and as the name suggests, it’s double the size of a float32.  Since float64’s are only 64 bits, it’s still considered a small datatype with some most modern processors spanning a 64-bit or 128-bit word length being able to handle many without the penalty of a larger data type (additional cache request, computation, etc.).

As machine learning workloads continue to achieve and even surpass gains from Moore’s law, heavy demand has been placed on hardware design to support a paradigm shift towards smaller data types.    There exist a plethora of smaller format datatypes that can be used in different applications including; Float16, BFloat16, Int, Int8, uInt8, Int16, Int32, Int64, complex, etc.  These data types are tuned and highly specific to certain operations, which translates to the deployment of Machine Learning workloads within ultra-streamlined devices such as smartphones and compact IoT interfaces, where weight, size, and power consumption are restricted.

BFloat16 — The BFloat16 datatype is similar to Float16 except it only uses 7 bits for the exponent (rather than 5 bits for float16), which allows for a larger dynamic range between exponent and exponent.  The larger dynamic range provides more than double the representational range compared to that of float16, providing a number of numeric advantages particularly for the various multiplication and division within DNN networks.  This advantageous dimension is also preserved in calculations, with more than one exponent bit being conserved in common operations.  The modelling advantages of BFloat16 are remarkably significant when viewing them through a stochastic lens, as issues arising with undesirably higher bias appear to be avoided, providing a noticeable lift in the unbiased representation of the data.  BFloat also improves variance in stochastic sampling due to hidden Z-members.

The other major advantage of BFloat16 is that it can exactly represent float32 multiplications and is capable of seamlessly translation between 32-bit and 16-bit computation, this ability enables future implementations that scale large models down to adapt to potentially hundreds of thousands of low power devices.

Float16 — Also known as half-precision, the Float16 has a 10-bit mantissa with 5 bits for the exponent and a total of 16 bits.  Compared to Float32 it has less precision, its representation of numbers tends to be a bit whacky at the edge seeing a wp of approximately 3 decimal digits vs 7 decimal digits but it consumes considerably fewer resources, less memory, fewer cache requests, fewer computations, and as addressed later has significant advantages over FP32 in terms of error handling on some large-scale acceleration demands.

