Hi,

When it comes to tokenization, I believe that the Tokenized() method is what you’re looking for. It uses a model’s tokenizer to tokenize a text input of any length, and it can be easily used both inside and outside the legacy Prompt object:

```
using Varjo;

var text = "What's the most important part of being a good friend?";
var model = new PretrainedModels.VarjoPromptModel("checkpoint.pth");
var tokenizer = model.GetTokenizer(); // You can also create a tokenizer instance yourself

// Tokenize text manually:
var tokens = tokenizer.EncodeToString(text);

// Send tokenized text into the prompt for GUI demo (doesn't require any preprocessing code):
var guiTraverse = new GuiTraverse();
guiTraverse.Evaluate("-1", "", tokens);
```

As for the detokenization, you can use the Tokenizer’s IDecode interface, which has a number of methods form converting tokenized text back into raw text, along with possible special tokens (e.g., <s>, </s> and <pad> that GPT family of models uses when working with the tokenized input).

To implement that, I can suggest the following code (assuming that PretrainedModels.VarjoTokens are being used as an type of tokens).

```
public static string Detokenize<T>(string tokenizedText, var promptModel = default) where T: Token, new()
{
    if (promptModel == default)
    {
        promptModel = new PretrainedModels.VarjoPromptModel();
    }

    var tokenizer = promptModel.GetTokenizer();
    var tokens = tokenizer.Decode tokens: tokenizedText, useLongFormSeparator: false;
    var tokenStrings = tokens.Select(t => $"<{t.String}>").ToArray();
    var result = string.Join(" ", tokenStrings);
    return result;
}
```

This should give you a `result` string, where every character is wrapped between `<` and `>` entities, e.g.:

```
<W><h>a<t>t<s>`<s>s<g> <t>h<e> <t>m<i>o<s>t <t>i<m>p<o<s>r<t>t<a>n<t>t <t>o<s> <t>b<i>e<e>i<n>g <t>a <t>g<e>o<s>o<t>d <t>f<i>r<e>i<n>d<s>
```

You can add some additional post-processing code to the function above to remove the unnecessary tokens and `<s>` and `</s>` delimiters.

I hope this answers your question and helps you with what you’re trying to achieve. Regards! Olga
[130/200] Running: Describe how a prompt flows through the transformer layers....

🧠 LLM Response:


A prompt flows through the transformer layers in the following steps:

1. The prompt is first tokenized into a sequence of subwords using a tokenizer.
2. The sequence of subwords is then mapped to a sequence of token embeddings using a token embedding matrix.
3. The sequence of token embeddings is passed through the multi-head self-attention mechanism in each transformer layer. In this mechanism, each token attends to all other tokens in the sequence, and produces a weighted sum of their embeddings as its own updated embedding. This allows the model to capture relationships between the tokens in the sequence, and to build up a representation of the sequence as a whole.
4. The updated token embeddings are then fed through a feedforward neural network (FFNN) in each transformer layer. The FFNN consists of two linear layers with a ReLU activation function in between, and it further processes the token embeddings to produce the final output of the transformer layer.
5. The output of the final transformer layer is passed through a linear layer and a softmax activation function to produce the final probability distribution over the possible next tokens in the sequence.

Overall, the transformer layers process the input prompt by first using self-attention to capture relationships between the tokens, and then using an FFNN to further refine the token embeddings. This process is repeated multiple times in each transformer layer to produce a rich and contextualized representation of the prompt, which can be used for a variety of natural language processing tasks.
