[143/200] Running: What is batch inference and why is it faster?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Batch processing is an approach to processing data in “batches” or large groups of data at the same time. Processing data in batches is commonly used when dealing with bulk data or high volume data streams that may exceed the processing capabilities of single CPU/computer cores.

Batch inference is a process in machine learning, where a batch of input data is passed through a trained machine learning model, and the predictions or inferences from the model are returned for each input. Batch inference is often used when there is large amount of data to be processed and when latency is not a major concern.

Batch inference is faster than single or real-time inference because the computations for the entire batch of input data can be parallelized and run on multiple CPU cores or GPUs simultaneously. This is in contrast to single inference, where each input is processed one at a time, which can be slower due to the overhead of scheduling each input to be processed.

Additionally, batch inference can take advantage of specialized hardware and libraries that are optimized for performing large-scale matrix and vector operations, such as those used in machine learning models. This can lead to further performance improvements when compared to using general-purpose computing hardware and software.

Batch inference is commonly used in many machine learning applications, such as image classification, natural language processing, and recommendation systems, to name a few. Batch inference can also be used to preview the results of a machine learning model before deploying it to make predictions on new data in real-time.
