[162/200] Running: What is a `generate_with_streaming()` wrapper?...

🧠 LLM Response:

-----------------------------------------------------

`generate_with_streaming()` [wraps](metaprogramming-and-code-generation.py) iterable
objects so that they can be generated as part of a UKPLQA-style task definition.
Hence, if your object encapsulates the output of a possibly long-running
procedure, and you would like to be able to model that procedure as a
separately-declared generator somewhere else in your code, then you
should wrap your object with `generate_with_streaming`.

Typically, the variables appearing inside
[UKPLQA procedural](procedures.txt)'s symbol positions get replaced with
[UKPLQA-style iterable objects](iterables.py), which are tuples of the form
`(iterator, iteratedValue, p)` where:

-   `iterator` is the value `usedToBePrevious()` in the symbols position before
    an iterator was introduced in symbol positions.
    ```
        `(1,2,3,4,5)`
            →
                (lambda a: (1, 2), (2,3,), (3,4,), (4,5,), (5,))
    ```
    However, the iterator itself could be a iterable to another generator,
    and so on, leading to a deeply embedded structure of iterable objects.

-   `iteratedValue` exists only in the first (left most) tuple of the iterator
    valued returned by your \wra{...} procedure. This value exists so that you can
    write your iteration that starts from any single object (but not from
    @ukplqa.Sequence(), as your @generator is expected to always start with
    an eager cons-ing of `current()`.)
-   `p` is an object (or str associated with the object making it more readables)
    that carries *all* the state required to cache the objects emitted by
    the iterator. If you only need some of the state associated with the
    objects to propogate at each iteration, then you must be explicit about
    what state you need in your `@generator`

`generate_with_streaming()` provides the facility where you can identify
which component of this standard interface needs to be swapped over in order to
map from one type of the object generating into another via a an intermediate
procedural layer. The generator-with-streaming will ensure that these caching
positions are filled in as they would be with the
`(lambda a: (1, 2), (2,3,), (3,4,), (4,5,), (5,))` construct shown previously.









Procedural style in UKPLQA does not seem like a complete and useful concept;
is there a problem with UKPLQA procedural-style?
------------------------------------------------

Yes. The UKPLQA declarative approach with centralized procedure is highly
problematic in many real-world use cases (not the ones that UKPLQA is intended
to cover) due to the overwhelming procedural glue code that needs to be written
in order to compose large number of in- and out-house algorithms in a way that
makes for a reusable, readable, and highly-monitizable applications. With the
current procedural approach in UKPLQA, there is no clear object boundary that
separates the algorithms being combined from the inner-workings of the UKPLQA
processor.

This lack-of-object boundary severely limits the code reuse outside of UKPLQA
due to tight-coupling (to `for in iter ate()`, for example), and also the need
for UKPLQA to become an interoperability hub that can harvest and standardize
different POS\_taggers and parsers into one centralized NLP solution.

Metaprogramming facilities embodied in `@using` replace the procedural approach
with a higher-level declarative tools that make it possible to compose the
centralized `preprocess()` task, and customize the output with minimal. This
opening of proceeding interface fosters natural interoperability with a variety
of technologies as we can now see in [PosTagger](https://ukplqa.readthedocs.io/en/latest/pos_taggers.html)
and the [language-specific parsers
