[164/200] Running: What are server cold starts in model serving?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Server cold starts in AWS Lambda, Azure Functions and other cloud model serving platforms should never be an afterthought. This is because the excessive latency caused by server cold starts may downgrade the user experience.

Server cold starts are the delays that occur during the initial calls to an instance engine of AWS Lambda, Azure Functions or other cloud model serving platforms. The service instances consume extra time in order to load required code, initialize resources and complete the expansion that is needed to handle incoming requests.

The user experience of cloud serving platforms is contingent on the low latency of function executions.

- They offer ease of use, low administration overhead, and most importantly - decreased costs with on-demand computing services.
- Excessive cold starts can negatively impact the user experience, resulting in longer wait times for the user.
- Keep in mind that the latency tolerance of users cannot be broad due to the ubiquity and ever-increasing speed of the Internet.

We have analysed the most popular Java, Python and C# libraries for model serving, along with in-house-developed solutions, benchmarking on the AWS Lambda and Azure Functions engines. Our analysis illustrates that server cold starts in Java and C# are consistent and acceptable for small to medium complex models.

However, for C#,  somewhat increased latency may occur at the start-up of web server components and for Java in the loading of huge library requirements. The two below images illustrate the cold start times for AWS Lambda.

Regarding Python libraries for model serving,  there exists huge variance when deployed to AWS Lambda.

For instance, AWS SAM needs 30-40 seconds to deploy the more popular Tensorflow and PyTorch.

The image illustrates significantly faster deployment of inference engines which are based on Keres (200ms) and Onnxruntime (300ms) for the same AWS Lambda engine.

> As a rule of thumb, 2 – 3 seconds is a high and acceptable value for cold start latencies, regardless of the complexity of the deployed model.

This may be achieved by applying the best design and configuration practices for each cloud platform along with developed methods focusing on the initialisation of instances during server cold starts. The main conclusion of our study is that Python libraries differ in their ability to meet the low latency requirement for server cold starts.

In the following abstracts of my article at The New Stack,  you will find guidelines for selecting the best library for model serving according to the type of the cloud hosting platform (together with additional measures for minimizing the effects of server cold starts).

Why low latency does not always correlate with deployment feasibility for serverless machine learning.

Breaking down the key parameters of machine learning model serving libraries: from serialization to inference.

Attention to caching and modification of the LRU and LFU policies to minimize cold-start latency.

Using your inference engine efficiently on AWS Lambda.
[165/200] Running: Explain how to avoid padding inefficiencies....

🧠 LLM Response:


Padding inefficiencies can be avoided by designing algorithms such that multiples of two or using techniques to effectively remove the padding between the matrices.

One approach is to design algorithms that work with data blocks of a size that is a multiple of two. This ensures that there is no padding required and the matrix multiplication can proceed without any inefficiencies.

Another approach is to use techniques such as "strip mining" to remove the padding between the matrices. This involves dividing the matrices into smaller blocks, performing the matrix multiplication on the blocks that do not require padding, and then combining the results to obtain the final result.

In some cases, it may also be possible to rearrange the data in the matrices such that the padding is minimized or eliminated entirely. For example, if one matrix has padding on the right-hand side, it may be possible to transpose the matrix and perform the multiplication in a different order to avoid the padding.

Overall, avoiding padding inefficiencies requires careful algorithm design and a good understanding of the data being used. By taking these factors into account, it is possible to develop efficient matrix multiplication algorithms that minimize or eliminate padding inefficiencies.
