[172/200] Running: How to debug slow streaming responses?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Here you can find tricks, techniques and useful tools to help to debug real-time or streaming HTTP responses and check if  they are slow due to your Node app or not.

Slow streaming responses can be tricky. We’re going to look here at how to debug your service, or the requesting agents which could be slowing down the exchange. This can seen either in the response time graph of the browser or the low streaming data flow.

We are going to see some tricks and techniques, and tools to debug these problems aiming for two possible scenarios:

- You want to check if your app is slow or if the medium of communication is slow
- You want to know what happens in the requesting agents

## Slow streaming response: is it my problem? Try Realtime Response Chrome Extension

Realtime Response stands for a local-based network measurement tool that is trying to measure the raw size and the amount of time it takes to fill your stream. You can take a look at it and understand it better on this post by Remy Sharp. Although if you’re tired, or too lazy to read the whole post, let me sum it up for you:

- It allows you to inject a header into the response called X-Stream-Size.
- It allows you to inject a header into the response called X-Stream-Duration.
- It helps you monitoring the received response with improved network throttling by dev tools.

So, then you’re going to insert these headers into your responses, and you’ll be able to check this info:

- The size of your data running for the stream (the serializado size). The range could be  between 0 and the X-Stream-Size header.
- The amount of time you think you’re sending information from the server to the browser based on the carried data.

This way, you’ll be able to tell if you’re not parsing the data before sending it, if you’re buffering data due to the fact of your message constructor not being serializable enough to be sent through the stream or if you’ve got some other queue problem.  It is also useful for end-to-end monitoring improvements.

## Slow streaming response: what’s going on with the client? Networking tools

The second approach to debug all these situations is using network APIs or tools. Let’s start by understanding that you’ll not only find the problem there, but you can also find its origin.

Debugging tools for testing network workflow performance via the http connection and the transport that carries your data are wireshark and tcpdump.  Using both tools, you can see which packets carry your data, how much information is moving and what is the e2e negotiated-at-time flow. You can check the carried data or the buffer of your packets, in addition to the rate of flow.

This could help you if, for example, on the network’s ingress grade, the whole amount of data you’re trying to send is being regulated in terms of its quantity (20 bytes per 10 sec, for example). With this argumentation you may find out that the browser is queuing on the JavaScript origin all the events of your streams in a race condition to deliver them across a massive packet instead of filling the receive buffer with your ongoing XHR requests.

You can also check the data that transport-related packages exhibit in the request streams with no problem. This can happen because your browser’s network stack is working on a massive Queue, and no stream, nor XHR request are blocked inside of the JavaScript event manager.

After checking this, we can conclude that our HTML is parsing too markup and our JS does many other things in the browser’s main thread, hence the Why our app is booting so slow arguments. Even then, there will be more conclusions or mileage based on the complexity of your app and the about of streams you’re starting and skimming from  all over each time you need a new stream for the sake of relevant information you want to show.

## More tools for slow streaming responses debugging

Mainly for debugging timebreaking flow issues, is to inspect on your browser(Chrome, Firefox) the WebSocket messages section with a Grid view. Checking the compression algorithm‘s switchover (payload vs. zipped size) for each message‘s payload plus checking the quantity of data that goes over the transport stack per request and even for recursive and non recursive streams’ events that could be being stacked for each XHR request, in the following message exchange.

